body:
### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

In the [update an element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#concept-upgrade-an-element) algorithm we have the next step and note:
> 3. Let constructResult be the result of [constructing](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#construct-a-callback-function) C, with no arguments.
> 
>
> > Note: If C [non-conformantly](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-conformance) uses an API decorated with the [[CEReactions]](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#cereactions) extended attribute, then the reactions enqueued at the beginning of this algorithm will execute during this step, before C finishes and control returns to this algorithm. Otherwise, they will execute after C and the rest of the upgrade process finishes.

I decided to test what is written in this note on current Chrome and Firefox:
```html
<a-elem a="100" m="200"></a-elem>
<script>
class A extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    console.log("start constructor");
    this.setAttribute("e", "5");
    console.log("end constructor");
  }

  attributeChangedCallback(name, old, value) {
    console.log("attributeChangedCallback:", name);
  }

  static get observedAttributes() {
    return ["a", "m", "e"]; 
  }
}

customElements.define("a-elem", A);
</script>
```

Based on the note, if reactions are queued before this constructor is called (that is, in [upgrade an element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#concept-upgrade-an-element)) and an API decorated with the [[CEReaction]](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#cereactions) attribute is called in the constructor, then this API will trigger all reactions until the constructor completes for the element for which the specific [[CEReaction]](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#cereactions) API is applied.

So, based on the note, the code I attached above should output the following:

- start constructor
- attributeChangedCallback: a
- attributeChangedCallback: m
- attributeChangedCallback: e
- end constructor

But in reality, if you run the code above, which uses `this.setAttribute("e", "5")` in the constructor, in Firefox or Chrome you'll see the following:

- start constructor
- end constructor
- attributeChangedCallback: a
- attributeChangedCallback: m

In fact, browser behavior follows the specification's logic, but the note, unfortunately, does not. Calling [setAttribute](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-element-setattribute) will not add the `attributeChangedCallback: e` reaction to the [custom element reaction queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-reaction-queue) for `this`. The [handle attribute changes](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#handle-attribute-changes) algorithm confirms this:
> 2. If element is [custom](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-element-custom), then [enqueue a custom element callback reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#enqueue-a-custom-element-callback-reaction) with element, callback name "attributeChangedCallback", and « attribute’s [local name](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-attribute-local-name), oldValue, newValue, attribute’s [namespace](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-attribute-namespace) ».

The element is not [custom](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-element-custom) at the time the constructor is run; it has the `"precustomized"` state. And as a consequence, the [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) for calling [setAttribute](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-element-setattribute) will be empty, which in turn means that [Invoke custom element reactions](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#invoke-custom-element-reactions) will not trigger the reactions pointed in the note (which are queued before the constructor in the [update an element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#concept-upgrade-an-element) operation), because there is no element in the [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) for which we [setAttribute](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-element-setattribute) was called.

I tracked down when this note was introduced, and [it was introduced in 2016](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/1366) and it seems to have a direct connection to [domenic's comment](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/1297#issuecomment-222891849):
> - For constructors that trigger CE reactions (in any way), the callbacks will fire at that time
>   - For an element with an existing attribute A whose constructor appends an attribute B, attributeChangedCallback will fire twice, in sequence A then B, immediately after the setAttribute call.

It seems that the note was written as a result of this comment, however, what I wrote above says the exact opposite.
